{% sec 1, "A Loan You Can Sell", "What is a bond, in plain words?", "A loan you can sell" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Say you lend a friend £100. They write you a signed note: "I'll pay you £5 every year for 10 years, then give you your £100 back." That note is a {% gl "bond" %}bond{% endgl %}. Now imagine you need your money back after three years. You can't ask your friend for it early, but you can sell the note to someone else, who will then receive the remaining payments. How much they'll pay you for it is the bond's price.</p>
    <p>Governments and companies borrow this way all the time, from thousands of lenders at once, and the notes trade between investors every day. Every bond has the same few parts:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="bp-anat">
    <div><b>Face value</b><span>The amount repaid at the end, also called par. Prices are quoted per 100 of face value.</span></div>
    <div><b>{% gl "coupon" %}Coupon{% endgl %}</b><span>The interest paid each year, as a percentage of face value. A \(5\%\) coupon on 100 pays 5 a year.</span></div>
    <div><b>Maturity</b><span>The date the face value is repaid and the bond ends. The time left is its tenor.</span></div>
    <div><b>Issuer</b><span>Who is borrowing: the UK government (gilts), the US government (Treasuries), Nigeria's government (FGN bonds), or a company.</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Some bonds pay no coupons at all. A {% gl "zero-coupon bond" %}zero-coupon bond{% endgl %} just pays its face value at maturity, so you buy it for less than 100 and the gap is your interest. Short-term government borrowing (Treasury bills in the UK, US and Nigeria) works this way.</p>
    <p>Bonds matter beyond the bond market: the yield on government bonds is the starting point for pricing mortgages, company loans, shares and property, and it's the market central banks influence when they do QE.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "The Price Is Today's Value of the Promises", "How do you work out what a bond is worth?", "Pricing a bond" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A bond is just a list of promised payments, so its price is what those payments are worth today. Discount each payment back to today at the market's interest rate for that kind of borrower (The Price of Money showed how) and add them up.</p>
    <p>That interest rate is the bond's {% gl "yield to maturity" %}yield to maturity{% endgl %}, usually just called its yield: the single rate that makes the discounted payments add up to exactly the price. It's the return you'll earn if you buy at today's price, hold to the end and every payment arrives.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example (Deltashark)</span>
    <h3>A 2-year zero-coupon bond, face value 100, at a \(5\%\) yield.</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>One payment: 100 in two years.</li>
      <li>Today's value: \(100 \div 1.05^2 = 100 \div 1.1025 = 90.70\).</li>
      <li>Check it forwards: \(90.70 \times 1.05 \times 1.05 = 100\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Paying \(90.70\) today for a certain 100 in two years <em>is</em> earning \(5\%\) a year. Price and yield are the same fact in two forms.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Build a bond's price</h3><p>Grey bars are the payments the bond promises (coupons, plus 100 at the end). Coloured bars are what each payment is worth today at the yield you set. The price is the coloured bars added together. Payments are once a year to keep it simple.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="presets bp-pick" id="cf-preset" aria-label="Example bonds"></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="cf-c">Coupon</label><input type="range" id="cf-c" min="0" max="25" step="0.1" value="5"><output id="cf-c-out" for="cf-c">5%</output></div>
